  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-017-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The reacceleration of globalization depended on transport standards, energy and institutions working together. Containerization reduced transfer costs across transport modes, while oil supplied dense energy for mobility, industry and logistics, allowing long-distance production networks to expand."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "These capabilities also create dependence. Disruption to routes, energy prices or key nodes can transmit costs to distant societies, so connection expands capacity while also widening the reach of local shocks."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article remains within the completed Book VI manuscript; B6-WEB-B does not add live current-data population. Subjective experience and structural conditions remain distinct, and no single measure is used to infer a person or an entire society."
        }
      ]
    }
